{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-17:111\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"17:111 repeats ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ, then grounds praise in God’s independence: He has no child, partner in dominion, or dependent ally. This gives the focus’s Rabb al-alamin a sovereign dimension.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:111\",\"ar\":\"وَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى لَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًۭا وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ شَرِيكٌۭ فِى ٱلْمُلْكِ وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ وَلِىٌّۭ مِّنَ ٱلذُّلِّ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact praise formula is followed by denials of dependence and shared rule.\",\"limits\":\"This parallel expands the grounds for praise; it does not define Rabb through those denials alone.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The opening of al-Kahf repeats ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ and names the sending of an unbent Book as its occasion. Read beside 1:2, praise of the Lord of all worlds includes praise for revealed guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنزَلَ عَلَىٰ عَبْدِهِ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ وَلَمْ يَجْعَل لَّهُۥ عِوَجَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages begin with the same praise formula; 18:1 immediately names the Book as a divine gift.\",\"limits\":\"The wording supports a shared praise frame, while 1:2 does not specify the Book as its object.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:27\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The Book is called the Book of the addressee’s Lord, whose words have no replacer. This gives a revealed-speech setting for the Rabb title in 1:2.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:27\",\"ar\":\"وَٱتْلُ مَآ أُوحِىَ إِلَيْكَ مِن كِتَابِ رَبِّكَ ۖ لَا مُبَدِّلَ لِكَلِمَٰتِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both use Rabb for God, and 18:27 joins that relation to recited revelation.\",\"limits\":\"The shared title supplies the link; the passages do not state that 1:2 refers specifically to this Book.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:38\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After recalling human creation, the speaker says Allah is his Lord and refuses to associate anyone with Him. The focus’s universal Rabb title is voiced here as personal allegiance and exclusive worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:37\",\"ar\":\"أَكَفَرْتَ بِٱلَّذِى خَلَقَكَ مِن تُرَابٍۢ ثُمَّ مِن نُّطْفَةٍۢ ثُمَّ سَوَّىٰكَ رَجُلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:38\",\"ar\":\"لَّٰكِنَّا۠ هُوَ ٱللَّهُ رَبِّى وَلَآ أُشْرِكُ بِرَبِّىٓ أَحَدًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names Allah as Rabb; the scene connects that title to creation and refusal of shirk.\",\"limits\":\"The personal confession does not itself state the universal scope expressed by al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:40\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The speaker expects his Lord may grant a better garden or send a disaster upon the existing one. This makes lordship concrete in control over livelihood and land.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:40\",\"ar\":\"فَعَسَىٰ رَبِّىٓ أَن يُؤْتِيَنِ خَيْرًۭا مِّن جَنَّتِكَ وَيُرْسِلَ عَلَيْهَا حُسْبَانًۭا مِّنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The garden’s fate is placed under the will of the speaker’s Rabb.\",\"limits\":\"This is a personal expectation in a particular dispute, not a direct gloss on al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:50\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Iblis breaks from his Lord’s command, and the passage warns against taking him and his descendants as allies. This supplies a sharp allegiance contrast beside the focus’s praise of the Lord of all worlds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:50\",\"ar\":\"فَفَسَقَ عَنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّهِۦٓ ۗ أَفَتَتَّخِذُونَهُۥ وَذُرِّيَّتَهُۥٓ أَوْلِيَآءَ مِن دُونِى وَهُمْ لَكُمْ عَدُوٌّۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same title Rabb appears in a scene where disobedience and rival allegiance are explicit.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not mention Iblis or allies; the relationship is a contrast in allegiance, not an identification of the passages’ scenes.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:82\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lord arranges for two orphans to reach maturity and recover their treasure, calling this mercy. The focus’s universal Rabb title can be heard through this particular mode of care and nurture.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:82\",\"ar\":\"فَأَرَادَ رَبُّكَ أَن يَبْلُغَآ أَشُدَّهُمَا وَيَسْتَخْرِجَا كَنزَهُمَا رَحْمَةًۭ مِّن رَّبِّكَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated Rabb and explicit mercy connect universal lordship to protection of vulnerable people.\",\"limits\":\"The episode illustrates one act of care; it does not exhaust the scope of al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:87\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The wrongdoer is returned to his Lord for punishment. In the Fatiha’s sequence, this scene gives an account of return and judgment after 1:2’s Rabb title and 1:4’s ملكِ يوم الدين.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:87\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ يُرَدُّ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِۦ فَيُعَذِّبُهُۥ عَذَابًۭا نُّكْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s immediate Fatiha context names the Day of Judgment; 18:87 depicts return to the Lord and punishment.\",\"limits\":\"The scene does not repeat the title Rabb al-alamin or the Fatiha’s wording for judgment.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:95\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"When offered payment to build a barrier, Dhu al-Qarnayn says what his Lord has granted is better and asks for help with strength. The focus’s Rabb title resonates with provision that enables public service rather than private payment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:94\",\"ar\":\"فَهَلْ نَجْعَلُ لَكَ خَرْجًا عَلَىٰٓ أَن تَجْعَلَ بَيْنَنَا وَبَيْنَهُمْ سَدًّۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:95\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ مَا مَكَّنِّى فِيهِ رَبِّى خَيْرٌۭ فَأَعِينُونِى بِقُوَّةٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The offer and reply set human payment against what the Lord has already granted.\",\"limits\":\"This episode offers one model of divinely granted capacity; it does not state that the focus refers to this act.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-18:109\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The sea would be exhausted before the words of the Lord ran out. This places the Lord named in 1:2 beyond the measure of the created world and its resources.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:109\",\"ar\":\"قُل لَّوْ كَانَ ٱلْبَحْرُ مِدَادًۭا لِّكَلِمَٰتِ رَبِّى لَنَفِدَ ٱلْبَحْرُ قَبْلَ أَن تَنفَدَ كَلِمَٰتُ رَبِّى\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages name God through Rabb; 18:109 contrasts the finite sea with inexhaustible divine words.\",\"limits\":\"The focus alone does not state that the Lord’s words are inexhaustible; 18:109 supplies that image.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-19:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The shared declaration Allah is my Lord and your Lord leads directly to worship and the straight path. This gives the focus’s Rabb of all worlds a communal consequence: the same Lord grounds a shared way of worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"19:36\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّ ٱللَّهَ رَبِّى وَرَبُّكُمْ فَٱعْبُدُوهُ ۚ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌۭ مُّسْتَقِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target pairs a shared Lord relation with worship and a path, terms that also structure the Fatiha after its praise.\",\"limits\":\"The target’s immediate speaker is addressing a particular community; the focus states the wider scope.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-19:65\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Lord of the heavens, earth, and what lies between them is followed by a command to worship and persevere in worship. This expands the cosmic scope of Rabb al-alamin into a concrete set of domains and a devotional response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"19:65\",\"ar\":\"رَّبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا فَٱعْبُدْهُ وَٱصْطَبِرْ لِعِبَٰدَتِهِۦ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use Rabb with an expansive cosmic domain; 19:65 draws worship from that claim.\",\"limits\":\"The target names cosmic regions rather than using al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-20:49\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Moses answers the question who their Lord is by describing the one who gives each thing its creation and then guides it. This supplies a compact account of Rabb al-alamin as creator and guide.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"20:49\",\"ar\":\"فَمَن رَّبُّكُمَا يَٰمُوسَىٰ\"},{\"ref\":\"20:50\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ رَبُّنَا ٱلَّذِىٓ أَعْطَىٰ كُلَّ شَىْءٍ خَلْقَهُۥ ثُمَّ هَدَىٰ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the Lord; the next verse answers a direct question about Rabb with creation and guidance.\",\"limits\":\"The answer is a prophetic statement in a confrontation with Pharaoh; it is not a grammatical definition of Rabb.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-21:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage names the sky as a protected ceiling, then says people turn away from its signs; the next verse lists night, day, sun, and moon in ordered motion. The focus’s al-alamin can be heard against this differentiated, sign-bearing cosmos and its ignored signs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"21:32\",\"ar\":\"وَجَعَلْنَا ٱلسَّمَآءَ سَقْفًۭا مَّحْفُوظًۭا ۖ وَهُمْ عَنْ ءَايَٰتِهَا مُعْرِضُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"21:33\",\"ar\":\"وَهُوَ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلَّيْلَ وَٱلنَّهَارَ وَٱلشَّمْسَ وَٱلْقَمَرَ ۖ كُلٌّۭ فِى فَلَكٍۢ يَسْبَحُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the Lord of all worlds; this passage displays celestial order and an audience turning away from its signs.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not use al-alamin or explicitly call the sky a world.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-21:56\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Ibrahim identifies the addressee’s Lord as Lord of the heavens and earth, their creator, and says he bears witness. This is a direct cosmic counterpart to the focus’s Lord of all worlds, placed in a stand against idol worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"21:56\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ بَل رَّبُّكُمْ رَبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ ٱلَّذِى فَطَرَهُنَّ وَأَنَا۠ عَلَىٰ ذَٰلِكُم مِّنَ ٱلشَّٰهِدِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target’s claim occurs amid a dispute about Ibrahim’s destruction of idols and names the creator as Lord.\",\"limits\":\"The target specifies heavens and earth rather than repeating al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After boarding the ark, Noah is told to say ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ because God saved them from the wrongdoers. The same praise formula as 1:2 is voiced as a response to deliverance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:28\",\"ar\":\"فَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى نَجَّىٰنَا مِنَ ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared formula is placed in a specific moment of rescue, followed by a prayer for a blessed landing.\",\"limits\":\"The focus gives no single rescue occasion; this passage supplies one instance of what praise can answer.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:72\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The target says the provision of your Lord is better than a possible human fee. It gives the Rabb title in 1:2 a material setting of sufficiency and provision.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:72\",\"ar\":\"أَمْ تَسْـَٔلُهُمْ خَرْجًۭا فَخَرَاجُ رَبِّكَ خَيْرٌۭ ۖ وَهُوَ خَيْرُ ٱلرَّٰزِقِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target names Rabb as provider in a dispute over payment.\",\"limits\":\"The relationship rests on the shared Rabb title and a provision theme, not on a repeated praise formula.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:93\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The speaker addresses God as Rabb while asking about a promised event and pleading not to be placed among wrongdoers. This is a personal supplication under the universal lordship named in 1:2.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:93\",\"ar\":\"قُل رَّبِّ إِمَّا تُرِيَنِّى مَا يُوعَدُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:94\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ فَلَا تَجْعَلْنِى فِى ٱلْقَوْمِ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target repeats Rabb in a first-person prayer, while the Fatiha moves from praise into requests.\",\"limits\":\"The shared devotional address alone does not establish a more specific link between the two prayers.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:98\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The speaker repeats Rabb while seeking refuge from the devils’ presence. Beside the Fatiha’s movement from praise to seeking help and guidance, this is another personal appeal to the Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:98\",\"ar\":\"وَأَعُوذُ بِكَ رَبِّ أَن يَحْضُرُونِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target combines the Rabb address with a request for protection; the Fatiha likewise turns from praise to reliance and petition.\",\"limits\":\"The target’s refuge request is not a quotation of the Fatiha’s wording.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:117\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage rejects invoking another deity and places the account with one’s Lord; it closes with a prayer for forgiveness and mercy. This links the focus’s praise of the Lord to exclusive worship, judgment, and dependence on mercy.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:116\",\"ar\":\"فَتَعَٰلَى ٱللَّهُ ٱلْمَلِكُ ٱلْحَقُّ ۖ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ رَبُّ ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْكَرِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:117\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يَدْعُ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَٰهًا ءَاخَرَ لَا بُرْهَٰنَ لَهُۥ بِهِۦ فَإِنَّمَا حِسَابُهُۥ عِندَ رَبِّهِۦٓ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:118\",\"ar\":\"وَقُل رَّبِّ ٱغْفِرْ وَٱرْحَمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The passage joins exclusive deity, Lordship, final accounting, and an appeal for mercy.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not spell out judgment or mercy; the surrounding passage supplies those dimensions.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:7\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:9\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:77\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The target asks what regard people would have with their Lord without their supplication. The Fatiha moves from praise in 1:2 to worship, seeking help, and asking for guidance; this target gives that prayerful turn human significance before the Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"25:77\",\"ar\":\"قُلْ مَا يَعْبَؤُا۟ بِكُمْ رَبِّى لَوْلَا دُعَآؤُكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The Fatiha’s praise opens into worship and petition; 25:77 makes supplication the stated ground of regard.\",\"limits\":\"The target does not specify that its دعاء means the Fatiha or praise in particular.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Moses and Aaron are sent to Pharaoh as messengers of the Lord of all worlds. The title in 1:2 thus appears as the source of a mission directed into political power and the demand to release the Children of Israel.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:15\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ كَلَّا ۖ فَٱذْهَبَا بِـَٔايَٰتِنَآ ۖ إِنَّا مَعَكُم مُّسْتَمِعُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:16\",\"ar\":\"فَأْتِيَا فِرْعَوْنَ فَقُولَآ إِنَّا رَسُولُ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:17\",\"ar\":\"أَنْ أَرْسِلْ مَعَنَا بَنِىٓ إِسْرَٰٓءِيلَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title is attached to a joint prophetic mission and a demand addressed to Pharaoh.\",\"limits\":\"The focus itself is praise, not a commission to any particular messenger.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:23\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Pharaoh asks what the Lord of all worlds is; the answer names the heavens, earth, and what lies between them. The focus’s compact title is unfolded as a claim that must be answered in a contested setting.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:23\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ فِرْعَوْنُ وَمَا رَبُّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:24\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ رَبُّ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَآ ۖ إِن كُنتُم مُّوقِنِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target repeats the focus’s exact title and immediately tests its meaning in dialogue.\",\"limits\":\"The answer offers one description of the Lord; it does not exhaust the title’s possible senses.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:47\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After the magicians fall in prostration, they say they believe in the Lord of all worlds. The focus’s title becomes the confession that follows a public contest over rival claims to power.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:46\",\"ar\":\"فَأُلْقِىَ ٱلسَّحَرَةُ سَٰجِدِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:47\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوٓا۟ ءَامَنَّا بِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:48\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ مُوسَىٰ وَهَٰرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title follows the magicians’ prostration and is then specified through Moses and Aaron.\",\"limits\":\"The confession occurs within this narrative contest; it is not itself a praise formula.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:48\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The magicians’ universal confession is immediately personalized as the Lord of Moses and Aaron. This joins the focus’s cosmic title to a specific prophetic relationship without narrowing its scope.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:47\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوٓا۟ ءَامَنَّا بِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:48\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ مُوسَىٰ وَهَٰرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target is the immediate clarification of the exact title in the preceding confession.\",\"limits\":\"The target names two prophets as servants of this Lord; it does not make them the limits of His lordship.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:77\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Abraham declares that the objects his people treat as enemies are excepted only by the Lord of all worlds; the next verse names creation and guidance. The focus’s title supplies the standard that separates the Creator from idols.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:77\",\"ar\":\"فَإِنَّهُمْ عَدُوٌّۭ لِّىٓ إِلَّا رَبَّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:78\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَنِى فَهُوَ يَهْدِينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact Lord of the worlds formula is set against the preceding catalogue of worshipped objects.\",\"limits\":\"Abraham’s personal account of creation and guidance develops the contrast but is not a full definition of al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:98\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The condemned speakers identify their error as having equated others with the Lord of all worlds. The focus’s title becomes the measure by which false equivalence is recognized.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:97\",\"ar\":\"تَٱللَّهِ إِن كُنَّا لَفِى ضَلَٰلٍۢ مُّبِينٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:98\",\"ar\":\"إِذْ نُسَوِّيكُم بِرَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target repeats the exact title as the standard their former worship violated.\",\"limits\":\"The passage gives the speakers’ retrospective confession, not a general account of every form of shirk.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:109\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Noah’s refrain says his reward is due only from the Lord of all worlds, after commanding his people to fear God and obey him. The focus’s title here underwrites prophetic service without payment from its audience.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:108\",\"ar\":\"فَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَأَطِيعُونِ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:109\",\"ar\":\"وَمَآ أَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ أَجْرٍ ۖ إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title appears as the sole source of reward in a prophetic call.\",\"limits\":\"This is Noah’s statement in one episode; the repeated refrain in this surah supplies the broader pattern.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:117\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Later in Noah’s episode, the people deny him and he appeals to his Lord, asking for a decisive judgment and rescue for the believers. Beside his earlier claim that his reward is with the Lord of all worlds, this forms a movement from uncompensated warning to dependence on divine intervention.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:109\",\"ar\":\"إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:117\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ رَبِّ إِنَّ قَوْمِى كَذَّبُونِ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:118\",\"ar\":\"فَٱفْتَحْ بَيْنِى وَبَيْنَهُمْ فَتْحًۭا وَنَجِّنِى وَمَن مَّعِىَ مِنَ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same prophet and surah carry the Lord relation from his opening call into a plea after rejection.\",\"limits\":\"The word al-alamin does not recur in 26:117; the link depends on the episode’s sequence.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:127\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Hud uses the same refrain that his reward is only with the Lord of all worlds, then challenges his people’s construction of monuments for amusement. The recurring title binds a different community’s moral correction to the shared prophetic claim.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:127\",\"ar\":\"وَمَآ أَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ أَجْرٍ ۖ إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:128\",\"ar\":\"أَتَبْنُونَ بِكُلِّ رِيعٍ ءَايَةًۭ تَعْبَثُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact refrain recurs in a distinct prophetic episode before a specific rebuke.\",\"limits\":\"The refrain alone does not explain the people’s building practices; the following verse supplies that setting.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:145\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Salih repeats the refrain of reward from the Lord of all worlds before asking whether his people expect to be left secure among their gardens, springs, and crops. The title links prophetic warning to their assumption of lasting safety and abundance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:145\",\"ar\":\"وَمَآ أَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ أَجْرٍ ۖ إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:146\",\"ar\":\"أَتُتْرَكُونَ فِى مَا هَٰهُنَآ ءَامِنِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared refrain precedes a challenge to presumed security in the community’s material setting.\",\"limits\":\"The excerpt does not state that their abundance itself is the cause of denial.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:164\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Lot uses the refrain of reward from the Lord of all worlds immediately before confronting his people’s sexual conduct. Its recurrence makes the same universal source of prophetic recompense frame a distinct moral dispute.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:164\",\"ar\":\"وَمَآ أَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ أَجْرٍ ۖ إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:165\",\"ar\":\"أَتَأْتُونَ ٱلذُّكْرَانَ مِنَ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target repeats the exact refrain, then specifies the community’s offense.\",\"limits\":\"The shared title frames Lot’s warning but does not by itself establish the passage’s legal or ethical reasoning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:165\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Immediately after the refrain about the Lord of all worlds, Lot asks whether they approach males from among the worlds, then invokes what their Lord created for them from their spouses. The repeated al-alamin puts human conduct within the focus’s broad language of worlds and creation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:164\",\"ar\":\"إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:165\",\"ar\":\"أَتَأْتُونَ ٱلذُّكْرَانَ مِنَ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:166\",\"ar\":\"وَتَذَرُونَ مَا خَلَقَ لَكُمْ رَبُّكُم مِّنْ أَزْوَٰجِكُم\"}],\"activation\":\"The target repeats the root and form al-alamin from 26:164, then turns to what the Lord created.\",\"limits\":\"The repeated noun does not by itself define the full range of al-alamin in 1:2.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:180\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Shuayb repeats the same claim that his reward is with the Lord of all worlds, then calls for honest measure and warns against shortchanging. The refrain joins universal lordship to a concrete commercial ethic in another community.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:180\",\"ar\":\"وَمَآ أَسْـَٔلُكُمْ عَلَيْهِ مِنْ أَجْرٍ ۖ إِنْ أَجْرِىَ إِلَّا عَلَىٰ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:181\",\"ar\":\"أَوْفُوا۟ ٱلْكَيْلَ وَلَا تَكُونُوا۟ مِنَ ٱلْمُخْسِرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The recurring exact title is followed by a distinct demand concerning trade.\",\"limits\":\"The target frames the demand but does not explicitly derive fair measure from the title alone.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:188\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Shuayb says his Lord knows what the people do; after their denial comes the punishment of the day of shadow, and the surah then names the Quran as revelation from the Lord of all worlds. This may connect divine knowledge of deeds, judgment, and revelation under the focus’s Lord title.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:188\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ رَبِّىٓ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا تَعْمَلُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:189\",\"ar\":\"فَكَذَّبُوهُ فَأَخَذَهُمْ عَذَابُ يَوْمِ ٱلظُّلَّةِ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:192\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّهُۥ لَتَنزِيلُ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The text gives a narrative transition from Shuayb’s episode to the revelation statement, but no explicit marker equates his personal Rabb with the title in 26:192. A clearer link between the prophetic warnings and the surah’s account of revelation could decide whether this chain is intentional.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:192\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After the surah’s sequence of prophetic confrontations and punishments, the Quran is identified as a sending-down from the Lord of all worlds, brought by the faithful Spirit. The focus’s title thus names the source of the revelation recited in the surah.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:191\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّ رَبَّكَ لَهُوَ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلرَّحِيمُ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:192\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّهُۥ لَتَنزِيلُ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:193\",\"ar\":\"نَزَلَ بِهِ ٱلرُّوحُ ٱلْأَمِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title occurs at the transition from prophetic histories to the account of Quranic revelation.\",\"limits\":\"The verse identifies the revelation’s source; it does not make the Fatiha the sole content meant by the Quran.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:8\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"At Moses’s encounter with the fire, the call glorifies Allah as Lord of all worlds before identifying Him as mighty and wise. The focus’s title therefore appears within a prophetic encounter as part of divine self-identification and glorification.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:8\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّا جَآءَهَا نُودِىَ أَنۢ بُورِكَ مَن فِى ٱلنَّارِ وَمَنْ حَوْلَهَا وَسُبْحَٰنَ ٱللَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:9\",\"ar\":\"يَٰمُوسَىٰٓ إِنَّهُۥٓ أَنَا ٱللَّهُ ٱلْعَزِيزُ ٱلْحَكِيمُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title is spoken in the scene that announces Moses’s encounter with Allah.\",\"limits\":\"The Fatiha is a human praise utterance; this scene presents a divine call to a prophet.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:15\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After describing knowledge given to David and Solomon, the surah quotes their praise of Allah for favoring them among believing servants. This gives the focus’s praise a particular epistemic form: knowledge received is acknowledged as gift rather than self-earned distinction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:15\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَا دَاوُۥدَ وَسُلَيْمَٰنَ عِلْمًۭا ۖ وَقَالَا ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى فَضَّلَنَا عَلَىٰ كَثِيرٍۢ مِّنْ عِبَادِهِ ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:16\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ هَٰذَا لَهُوَ ٱلْفَضْلُ ٱلْمُبِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same praise formula follows a stated divine gift of knowledge.\",\"limits\":\"The target gives a specific reason for these two prophets’ praise; 1:2 leaves its grounds open.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage moves from prostration to Allah, who brings forth hidden things and knows what people conceal, to Allah as Lord of the mighty throne. This offers a throne-centered counterpart to the focus’s universal Rabb title within a dispute about whom to worship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:25\",\"ar\":\"أَلَّا يَسْجُدُوا۟ لِلَّهِ ٱلَّذِى يُخْرِجُ ٱلْخَبْءَ فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَيَعْلَمُ مَا تُخْفُونَ وَمَا تُعْلِنُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:26\",\"ar\":\"ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ رَبُّ ٱلْعَرْشِ ٱلْعَظِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The nearby passage supplies exclusive worship, cosmic action, and a Rabb title with a different stated domain.\",\"limits\":\"The target says Lord of the mighty throne, not Lord of all worlds; the passages do not equate those expressions.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:42\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The scene moves from the queen’s tentative recognition of her altered throne to the explicit confession in 27:44 that she has wronged herself and submits to Allah, Lord of all worlds. The focus’s title is the endpoint of a narrated recognition, not merely a given formula.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"27:42\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّا جَآءَتْ قِيلَ أَهَٰكَذَا عَرْشُكِ ۖ قَالَتْ كَأَنَّهُۥ هُوَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:43\",\"ar\":\"وَصَدَّهَا مَا كَانَت تَّعْبُدُ مِن دُونِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:44\",\"ar\":\"قَالَتْ رَبِّ إِنِّى ظَلَمْتُ نَفْسِى وَأَسْلَمْتُ مَعَ سُلَيْمَٰنَ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target sits in the recognition sequence that culminates in the exact title from 1:2.\",\"limits\":\"The target’s tentative answer alone does not state the final confession; the next verses complete the movement.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:44\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After misreading the glass floor as water, the queen admits she wronged herself and submits with Solomon to Allah, Lord of all worlds. The focus’s exact title appears here as the outcome of corrected perception and a change of allegiance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:44\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ إِنَّهُۥ صَرْحٌۭ مُّمَرَّدٌۭ مِّن قَوَارِيرَ ۗ قَالَتْ رَبِّ إِنِّى ظَلَمْتُ نَفْسِى وَأَسْلَمْتُ مَعَ سُلَيْمَٰنَ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title closes a narrative in which the queen’s prior worship and perception are called into question.\",\"limits\":\"The scene shows this queen’s confession; it does not prescribe one route by which every person recognizes the Lord.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:59\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to proclaim ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ is immediately followed by a question contrasting Allah with what people associate with Him; the next verse points to creation and provision. The focus’s praise formula thus opens into a polemic that tests rival worship against the world’s signs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:59\",\"ar\":\"قُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ وَسَلَٰمٌ عَلَىٰ عِبَادِهِ ٱلَّذِينَ ٱصْطَفَىٰٓ ۗ ءَآللَّهُ خَيْرٌ أَمَّا يُشْرِكُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:60\",\"ar\":\"أَمَّنْ خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ وَأَنزَلَ لَكُم مِّنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءًۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The target repeats the praise formula and immediately asks whether Allah or the associated objects are better.\",\"limits\":\"The focus itself contains no direct polemic against other worship; the surrounding passage supplies it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-27:93\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After the command to recite the Quran, the messenger is told to say ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ; the verse then promises signs people will recognize and says the Lord is not unaware of their deeds. The focus’s praise is placed beside recognition and accountability.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:92\",\"ar\":\"وَأَنْ أَتْلُوَا۟ ٱلْقُرْءَانَ\"},{\"ref\":\"27:93\",\"ar\":\"وَقُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ سَيُرِيكُمْ ءَايَٰتِهِۦ فَتَعْرِفُونَهَا ۚ وَمَا رَبُّكَ بِغَٰفِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same praise formula follows Quranic recitation and precedes signs and the Lord’s knowledge of deeds.\",\"limits\":\"The target addresses a messenger and audience in a specific warning; 1:2 does not name that occasion.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:30\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"At the bush, the voice identifies itself as Allah, Lord of all worlds. The title in 1:2 is thus spoken here as divine self-identification in a prophetic encounter, anchoring the extraordinary scene in the universal Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"28:30\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّآ أَتَىٰهَا نُودِىَ مِن شَٰطِئِ ٱلْوَادِ ٱلْأَيْمَنِ فِى ٱلْبُقْعَةِ ٱلْمُبَٰرَكَةِ مِنَ ٱلشَّجَرَةِ أَن يَٰمُوسَىٰٓ إِنِّىٓ أَنَا ٱللَّهُ رَبُّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact title occurs in the divine address to Moses.\",\"limits\":\"The focus is a praise utterance; this verse is an act of self-identification within a prophetic call.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:70\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The target says that praise belongs to God in the first life and the hereafter, then names His judgment and the return to Him. This extends the focus’s attribution of praise across time as well as across the worlds named in 1:2.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"28:70\",\"ar\":\"وَهُوَ ٱللَّهُ لَآ إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا هُوَ ۖ لَهُ ٱلْحَمْدُ فِى ٱلْأُولَىٰ وَٱلْءَاخِرَةِ ۖ وَلَهُ ٱلْحُكْمُ وَإِلَيْهِ تُرْجَعُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages assign praise to Allah; 28:70 explicitly spans the first life and the hereafter.\",\"limits\":\"The temporal scope in 28:70 and the worlds in 1:2 are distinct expressions of scope.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:50\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The people demand signs from their Lord; the reply says signs belong to Allah and defines the messenger as a clear warner. The nearby claim that the recited Book suffices may connect the focus’s Lord title to revelation and the limits of a messenger’s control over signs.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:50\",\"ar\":\"وَقَالُوا۟ لَوْلَآ أُنزِلَ عَلَيْهِ ءَايَٰتٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّهِۦ ۖ قُلْ إِنَّمَا ٱلْءَايَٰتُ عِندَ ٱللَّهِ وَإِنَّمَآ أَنَا۠ نَذِيرٌۭ مُّبِينٌ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:51\",\"ar\":\"أَوَلَمْ يَكْفِهِمْ أَنَّآ أَنزَلْنَا عَلَيْكَ ٱلْكِتَٰبَ يُتْلَىٰ عَلَيْهِمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"The focus contains no messenger, sign, or Book language. A clearer Quranic link between Rabb al-alamin and the authority over revealed signs could establish whether this is more than a shared divine-source frame.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:63\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Asked who sends rain and revives the earth, the people answer Allah; the messenger is then commanded to say ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ, followed by the observation that most do not reason. The focus’s praise formula is here a response to recognized life-giving action, while recognition alone does not ensure understanding.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:63\",\"ar\":\"وَلَئِن سَأَلْتَهُم مَّن نَّزَّلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءًۭ فَأَحْيَا بِهِ ٱلْأَرْضَ مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَوْتِهَا لَيَقُولُنَّ ٱللَّهُ ۚ قُلِ ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ ۚ بَلْ أَكْثَرُهُمْ لَا يَعْقِلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The exact praise formula follows acknowledgment of Allah’s life-giving act and precedes a rebuke about reasoning.\",\"limits\":\"The target names one sign of provision; it does not reduce all praise in 1:2 to that event.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:18\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The target says praise belongs to God in the heavens and earth, then follows with the cycle of life emerging from death and the reviving of dead earth. This gives a spatially explicit counterpart to the focus’s Lord of all worlds and ties cosmic praise to life-giving power.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"30:18\",\"ar\":\"وَلَهُ ٱلْحَمْدُ فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَعَشِيًّۭا وَحِينَ تُظْهِرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"30:19\",\"ar\":\"يُخْرِجُ ٱلْحَىَّ مِنَ ٱلْمَيِّتِ وَيُخْرِجُ ٱلْمَيِّتَ مِنَ ٱلْحَىِّ وَيُحْىِ ٱلْأَرْضَ بَعْدَ مَوْتِهَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages assign praise to God across a cosmic scope; 30:19 supplies examples of life-giving action.\",\"limits\":\"The target names heavens and earth and times of day, rather than using al-alamin.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
